WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.066
Most Rust code uses lifetimes without
writing explicit lifetime annotations.

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:07.818
The compiler can infer many relationships
from ownership, borrowing, and scope.

3
00:00:07.898 --> 00:00:11.411
That is why common functions can accept
references and return ordinary values

4
00:00:11.491 --> 00:00:13.726
without becoming cluttered with lifetime
names.

5
00:00:13.806 --> 00:00:16.414
Lifetime elision rules keep common
signatures readable.

6
00:00:16.494 --> 00:00:18.304
They do not remove the underlying model.

7
00:00:18.384 --> 00:00:22.216
They simply allow developers to omit
lifetime names when the relationship is obvious

8
00:00:22.296 --> 00:00:24.478
enough for the compiler to apply standard
rules.

9
00:00:24.558 --> 00:00:27.858
The safety check is still present even
when the annotation is not written.

10
00:00:27.938 --> 00:00:31.717
Explicit annotations become useful when a
function has multiple references and the

11
00:00:31.797 --> 00:00:34.352
relationship between inputs and outputs
must be stated.

12
00:00:34.432 --> 00:00:38.051
For example, a function may return a
reference that is tied to one specific input.

13
00:00:38.131 --> 00:00:40.632
In that case, the annotation is part of
the API contract.

14
00:00:40.712 --> 00:00:43.533
The key lesson is that annotations do not
make data live longer.

15
00:00:43.613 --> 00:00:46.327
They document a relationship the code must
already satisfy.

16
00:00:46.407 --> 00:00:48.802
If a local value is cleaned up when a
function returns,

17
00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:52.289
no lifetime annotation can make a
reference to that local value valid for the

18
00:00:52.369 --> 00:00:52.741
caller.

19
00:00:52.821 --> 00:00:54.950
Defensive Rust avoids lifetime cargo
culting.

20
00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:58.277
Do not add lifetime names mechanically
just to satisfy a compiler error.

21
00:00:58.357 --> 00:01:00.486
Ask what relationship the API needs to
express.

22
00:01:00.566 --> 00:01:03.653
If there is no valid borrowed owner for
the caller, return owned data.

23
00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:06.660
If the relationship is real, make it clear
through the signature.
